Fillets: a boss's top rim rounded down to its foot's earlier rounding,
where the foot has one: the rim's contact on the wall is split where it
runs along that rounding's edge, so that the wall keeps no strip of no
width there (mitcad#1419).

The case: a cylindrical boss of radius 5 stands 2 high on a plate that
covers only part of its foot; past the plate's side the wall runs on down
along it. The foot, an arc where the plate is, is rounded with 0.5; the
top rim, a whole circle, is then rounded with 1.5. The rim's contact on
the wall, a circle at the height 0.5, runs along the foot rounding's edge
on the wall where the plate is, and on inside the wall past that edge's
ends. The analytic blend (a torus, SplitKPart) is one piece along the
whole circle: 0014 hatches a line on its face's boundary a little inside
the face, and the whole circle is one domain. The wall was then cut along
the whole circle and kept the strip between the circle and the foot
rounding's edge, of no width: its wire held both and intersected itself
(BRepCheck_SelfIntersectingWire), the shape was not valid, and the
material taken was 5e-3 mm3 short of the rounding's (14.152809 mm3). The
closed contact's start on the wall's seam was moreover cut at the
parameter of the line hatched inside the face (1e-4 above the contact),
so that the seam's piece went on past the contact's point and the wall's
wire had a gap of 1e-4 there.

- edgeEndOnContact (new): a vertex V of the contact's face strictly inside
  the contact, on it, where exactly one of the face's edges at V (not of
  the spine) lies wholly on the contact, its other vertex on the contact
  too, and the contact past V away from that edge lies inside the face.
- ChFi3d_ContactsAlongFaceEdges splits a SurfData at such a vertex (as at
  0014's inner vertices of a chain along the contact,
  ChFi3d_SplitAtVertexOnLine): the part along the edge is then a contact
  along a whole edge, stored as a blend on that edge with the face beyond
  it (the foot rounding), the others inside the face up to the vertices.
  Both roundings meet along the edge, and the wall keeps only its part
  past the plate's side.
- FillSD: an end of a line hatched on its face's boundary (0014) that
  stays on an arc away from the arc's vertices takes the parameter of its
  own point on the arc, not that of the line moved inside the face.

On the reported design's stored body before the item, the rounding takes
14.152982 mm3 against the file's 14.152911 (5e-6 relative; before
14.147664 and not valid), its new faces on the file's within 1e-6 mm.

